        ],
        "summary": "Fixed the issue where having several start-end tags in an included page resulted in only the first appearance being included.\n\n**Root cause:** The original code used `str.partition()` which only finds the first matching start and end tag pair, discarding any subsequent pairs.\n\n**Solution:** Created a new `_process_start_end_pairs()` function in `mkdocs_include_markdown_plugin/event.py` that:\n- Handles all combinations of start/end tag options (neither, only start, only end, both)\n- When both start and end tags are specified, loops through ALL start-end pairs in the text, extracting content between each pair\n- When only an end tag is specified, includes content before the first end tag\n- When only a start tag is specified, includes content after the first start tag until end of text\n\n**Changes made to `mkdocs_include_markdown_plugin/event.py`:**\n1. Added `_process_start_end_pairs()` function\n2. Modified `found_include_tag()` to use the new function instead of `partition()`\n3. Modified `found_include_markdown_tag()` to use the new function instead of `partition()`\n\n**Test results:** All 40 tests pass, including the two `multiple-start-end-matchs` tests that were previously failing.",
        "turns": 52
      }
    }
